# JANET configuration — copy to `.env` and fill in what you use.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored: it holds real passwords and tokens, so it must never be
# committed. Everything here is OPTIONAL — JANET's core (listening, transcribing,
# intents, timers, alarms, maths, the local LLM) needs none of it. These keys only
# switch on the integrations that reach outside the machine.
#
# Every integration also has a "demo" backend: a fake, in-memory version with
# believable sample data. Set the `=demo` keys below to try JANET's calendar,
# weather, smart-home and email out with no accounts, no servers, no credentials.
# That's also how the automated checks run.


# ─── The language model (JANET's voice) ──────────────────────────────────────
# JANET speaks through an LLM: handlers produce facts, the model turns them into
# what you actually hear. It talks over the OpenAI-compatible /v1 HTTP API, so
# the backend is just a URL and there's no client library to install.
#
# Default is a llama.cpp server, which is worth knowing about: if you run
# llama-server with the model fully on the GPU (-ngl 999), JANET reuses those
# loaded weights. Running Ollama alongside it loads a SECOND copy into whatever
# VRAM is left, which pushes it onto the CPU and makes every reply 3-5x slower.
#
#JANET_LLM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081/v1      # llama.cpp (default)
#JANET_LLM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1     # Ollama instead
#JANET_LLM_MODEL=qwen3-14b                   # ignored by single-model llama.cpp

# Let JANET escalate itself to full step-by-step thinking on hard questions.
# It says something casual first ("hang on, let me think about that one") so the
# pause isn't dead air. OFF by default: it takes ~11-20s, and while the mic does
# keep listening throughout (capture is its own thread), JANET can't answer you
# again until it finishes.
#JANET_DEEP_THINKING=1

# How long after JANET speaks a short follow-up ("and tomorrow?") still counts as
# part of the conversation. Inside this window JANET asks the LLM whether an
# otherwise-ignored utterance was meant for it; outside it, nothing changes and
# ambient speech costs nothing. Default 30 seconds.
#JANET_FOLLOWUP_WINDOW=30


# Save every captured utterance to data/debug_audio/ as a WAV, with its duration
# printed. Use this when JANET keeps mishearing you: it separates "the mic only
# caught a fragment" (a VAD/level problem) from "Whisper mangled a good
# recording" (a model problem). Gitignored — these are recordings of your voice.
#JANET_DEBUG_AUDIO=1

# Whisper model. 'small' by default; 'tiny' is faster to load and less accurate.
#JANET_WHISPER_MODEL=small


# ─── Calendar ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pick ONE: demo, google, or CalDAV (leave JANET_CALENDAR unset for CalDAV).

# The fake calendar — sample events seeded around "now". No server needed.
#JANET_CALENDAR=demo

# CalDAV. Point this at a self-hosted Radicale/Nextcloud and it stays fully local.
# All three keys are required or JANET reports the calendar as not set up.
#JANET_CALDAV_URL=https://example.com/dav/
#JANET_CALDAV_USER=you
#JANET_CALDAV_PASSWORD=app-specific-password

# Google Calendar. One-time setup:
#   1. Google Cloud console -> new project -> enable the Google Calendar API
#   2. OAuth consent screen -> External -> add yourself as a test user
#   3. Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Desktop app -> download the JSON
#   4. Save it to the path below, then run the consent ONCE from a terminal
#      (never mid-conversation — it opens a browser and would block the mic):
#         cd src && ../venv/bin/python -c \
#           "from integrations.google_calendar_source import authorize; authorize()"
#JANET_CALENDAR=google
#JANET_GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS=~/.config/janet/google_credentials.json
#JANET_GOOGLE_TOKEN=~/.config/janet/google_token.json


# ─── Weather ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Uses Open-Meteo, which needs NO API key. Give it either a city (geocoded for
# you) or explicit coordinates. Temperatures are Celsius.

#JANET_WEATHER=demo
#JANET_WEATHER_CITY=Pune
#JANET_WEATHER_LAT=18.5196
#JANET_WEATHER_LON=73.8553


# ─── Smart home (Home Assistant) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Home Assistant on your own LAN, so this stays local. The token is a
# long-lived access token from your HA profile page.
# Only lights, switches and fans are controllable — locks, garage doors and
# alarm panels are deliberately excluded and cannot be voice-controlled.

#JANET_SMART_HOME=demo
#JANET_HA_URL=http://homeassistant.local:8123
#JANET_HA_TOKEN=your-long-lived-access-token


# ─── Email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reading is IMAP and uses BODY.PEEK, so JANET checking your mail never marks
# it as read. Gmail/Outlook need an APP-SPECIFIC password, not your login one.

#JANET_EMAIL=demo
#JANET_IMAP_HOST=imap.gmail.com
#JANET_IMAP_USER=you@example.com
#JANET_IMAP_PASSWORD=app-specific-password
#JANET_IMAP_PORT=993

# Sending is only used to REPLY to the message you just heard about, and always
# asks for a spoken confirmation first. Leave these unset to keep JANET
# read-only; it will say "Sending isn't set up yet."
#JANET_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
#JANET_SMTP_PORT=465
#JANET_SMTP_USER=you@example.com
#JANET_SMTP_PASSWORD=app-specific-password
#JANET_EMAIL_FROM=you@example.com

# Keep the microphone live while JANET is speaking.
# ONLY useful with echo cancellation loaded, or JANET transcribes its own voice
# and answers itself:  pactl load-module module-echo-cancel
#JANET_BARGE_IN=1

# --- Text-to-speech (src/audio/tts.py) ---
# JANET speaks with Piper, a local neural synthesiser. Nothing here is required.
#
# Which synthesiser to use. `espeak` forces the old robotic formant voice —
# useful when porting to another OS, when debugging audio routing, or if you
# don't want the voice model on disk. Anything else means Piper.
#JANET_TTS=espeak
#
# Which Piper voice. The default was chosen by ear from six candidates.
# List every voice (run with NO arguments; 38 of them are English):
#   ./venv/bin/python -m piper.download_voices | grep '^en_'
# The model downloads once, on first run, to the directory below (~63 MB).
# JANET_NO_DOWNLOAD=1 blocks that, as it does for the intent classifier.
#JANET_PIPER_VOICE=en_US-amy-medium
#JANET_PIPER_VOICE_DIR=~/.local/share/piper-voices
#
# Speaking rate, as a duration multiplier: LOWER IS FASTER. Unset means "the
# pace the voice was trained at". This rescales phoneme durations before the
# vocoder runs, so pitch and timbre are unaffected — it is not the same as
# speeding up a finished recording. Try 0.85 if the default feels slow.
#JANET_PIPER_SPEED=0.85

# --- Addressing gate (src/intent/nli_scorer.py) ---
# Decides whether you were talking to JANET at all, by reading the last few
# turns of the conversation. Nothing here is required; the defaults are the
# measured ones. Re-measure any change with:
#   ./venv/bin/python tools/addressing_eval.py --extra-negatives --live
#
# The default checkpoint is ~1.7GB and downloads from Hugging Face on FIRST RUN,
# so JANET needs starting once with a network connection before it will work
# offline. It caches under ~/.cache/huggingface.
#JANET_NLI_MODEL=MoritzLaurer/deberta-v3-large-zeroshot-v2.0
# Smaller and weaker, if VRAM is tight — 379 MiB instead of 848 MiB:
#JANET_NLI_MODEL=MoritzLaurer/deberta-v3-base-zeroshot-v2.0

# The probability at which an utterance counts as addressed. Lower it to make
# JANET more talkative, raise it to make it interrupt less.
#JANET_NLI_CUT=0.20

# How many previous exchanges the gate reads. One turn = one you+JANET pair.
# NOTE: the conversation is only shown to the model when JANET's own last reply
# ended in a question. Showing it every time made ambient speech spoken DURING
# a conversation score 0/16 -- an Assistant turn in the premise nearly entails
# "they are talking to the assistant" all by itself. Follow-ups after a
# statement are caught by the LLM rescue instead. See intent/nli_scorer.py.
#JANET_NLI_TURNS=5

# Keyword score that may overrule the model on its own. In practice this means
# "your name AND a real intent word". It exists because the model is weakest on
# bare commands like "turn on the kitchen lights", which keywords nail.
#JANET_NLI_STRONG=90
